<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Port-au-Prince</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Port-au-Prince is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, on a bay at the eastern end of the Gulf of Gonâve. Founded by the French in 1749, it became the seat of government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in 1770 and has been Haiti's capital for most of the time since independence in 1804. Much of the city was destroyed by the earthquake of January 2010, and in the 2020s armed gangs took control of large parts of it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/port-au-prince.jpg" alt="Port-au-Prince" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo by Mass Communication Specialist 2nd Class Meranda Keller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_(cropped2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since 2020 the state has lost its monopoly of force: gangs held about 80% of <b>Port-au-Prince</b> by late 2023, killed 4,789 people and kidnapped 2,490 that year, and neither the police, bled by desertion, nor the Kenyan-led Multinational Security Support Mission has restored public safety.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Haiti arose from the crisis that followed the assassination of Emperor Jacques I, the former Jean-Jacques Dessalines, near <b>Port-au-Prince</b> on 17 October 1806.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the top of colonial society stood the grands blancs, the “big whites”: the owners of the great sugar estates, the leading merchants of Cap-Français and <b>Port-au-Prince</b>, and the senior officials and officers.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li><li><blockquote>The anthem was first performed in October 1903, at a celebration of the Armée Indigène's entry into <b>Port-au-Prince</b> organised by the Association du Petit Théâtre, sung by Auguste de Pradines, known as Kandjo.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-dessalinienne">La Dessalinienne</a></li><li><blockquote>A magnitude 7.0 earthquake hit near <b>Port-au-Prince</b>, destroying much of the capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-12">12 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>British troops from Jamaica landed at Jérémie and Môle Saint-Nicolas in September 1793 and captured <b>Port-au-Prince</b> in June 1794.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-saint-domingue">British occupation of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
